<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sunda Strait</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sunda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sunda Strait is the channel between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> connecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> was positioned to control shipping through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and Sunda straits. It was a key route for maritime trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sunda-strait.jpg" alt="Sunda Strait" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Telim tor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunda_strait_map_v4.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Palembang on the Musi River in southern Sumatra was the empire's core capital, strategically placed to control shipping moving between the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea through the Malacca and <b>Sunda</b> straits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The stone pillar set up for <b>Sunda</b>’s 1522 treaty with Portugal was rediscovered in Jakarta in 1918 and is now kept in the National Museum of Indonesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The volcano of Krakatoa in the <b>Sunda Strait</b> began its climactic eruption, culminating in explosions the next day heard thousands of kilometres away.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition says Prince Walangsungsang, son of the <b>Sunda</b> king, founded a settlement there around 1430 and embraced Islam.</blockquote><a href="/state/cirebon-sultanate">Cirebon Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>When Banten destroyed Pakuan Pajajaran in 1579, <b>Sunda</b> nobles are said to have carried the royal crown, the Binokasih, to Sumedang.</blockquote><a href="/state/sumedang-larang">Sumedang Larang</a></li></ul></section>
